we asked Americans about their  favorability towards private and public  institutions first we found that  Americans tend to prefer private sector  firms to public sector firms Americans  also tend to prefer institutions that  let them choose between the firm's that  they use and what I mean by that is that  people can switch grocery stores people  can switch the bank that they go to it  gets harder to switch your local  government or your state government and  it becomes even harder to switch your  federal government and this probably  explains why eighty eight percent of  Americans have a favorable impression of  their local grocery store whereas only  thirty-three percent have a favorable  impression of the federal government so  with the 2012 presidential election  approaching we have several GOP  presidential candidates talking about  abolishing and consolidating that the  federal bureaucracy Americans are fairly  evenly divided about whether they would  like to abolish and consolidate the  Department of Housing and Urban  Development or keep it as it is with  forty four percent in favor of Allah  Shing and forty-seven percent oppose and  this is especially interesting because  despite its existence we've still had a  housing boom and bust in general a  majority of Americans aren't quite ready  to say they want to abolish these  federal departments what we did find  though is that between thirty to forty  five percent of Americans do favor  abolishing and consolidating these  departments so it's hard to argue that  wanting to abolish I'm in consolidate  these federal departments is fringe or  way outside the mainstream reeling from  the onset of the 2008 financial crisis  politicians and political candidates  seem to rally around a similar mantra in  which they said we might not agree about  what we should do but we can agree that  government needs to do something so in  2011 we asked Americans what were they  more concerned about were they more  concerned about government failing to  take action or were they more  that government would take action but  that action would make things worse and  we found that a majority of Americans  fifty two percent actually are most  concerned that the government will take  action but that action will make things  worse we also surveyed Americans  willingness to consider specific  third-party candidates who might enter  the 2012 general election of twenty one  percent of americans said that they  would or might consider voting for  former new mexico governor gary johnson  we also asked about congressman ron paul  thirty-five percent of americans said  they would or might consider voting for  him in the general election and  thirty-three percent said they would  consider voting for new york mayor  michael bloomberg  you
 

